<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taika Reform</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/645">645</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Taika Reform was a programme of political reforms begun in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 645. Inspired by Chinese models, it sought to strengthen the imperial court, bring land and people under state control and create a centralised administration. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a> present it as an example of how Tang governance served as a template for state-building elsewhere in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the regency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-shotoku">Shōtoku Taishi</a> ended, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soga-clan">Soga clan</a>, from which Shōtoku's ancestry came, took control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamato-court">Yamato court</a>. The clan opposed Shōtoku's son Yamashiro Ōe and killed him in 643. Under Empress Kōgyoku the Soga head, Soga no Iruka, was virtually an all-powerful leader of the court. The emperor's brother Karu, the emperor's son Prince Naka no Ōe, his friend Nakatomi no Kamatari and Iruka's cousin Soga no Ishikawamaro opposed him, and they ended his regime in a coup in 645 known as the Isshi incident. Kōgyoku gave up the throne and Karu became Emperor Kōtoku.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Kōtoku and Prince Naka no Ōe issued a series of reform measures that culminated in the Taika Reform Edicts of 646. Two scholars who had travelled to Sui China with Ono no Imoko, Takamuko no Kuromaro and the priest Min, were made national doctors and probably took a major part in drafting them. The ruler was no longer a clan leader but an emperor, or Tennō, with absolute authority, and the edicts abolished the old kabane system of hereditary titles.</p><p>The country was reorganised into 66 provinces and 592 counties under appointed governors. Subjects were to be surveyed, land nationalised and redistributed, and private weapons stored in government armouries. A land tax, military service and labour duties were imposed on subjects. A permanent capital was established at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a>, modelled on the grid of the Tang capital. A new central government included a chancellor, the Daijō daijin, who presided over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-council-qing">Grand Council</a> of State, and an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-examination">examination system</a>, open only to nobles. The role of women became much more restricted, especially in official life.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The changes unfolded in successive steps over many years, and a major later step was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiho-code">Taihō Code</a> of 702, with criminal and administrative laws modelled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>, which led to the ritsuryō system. The edicts curtailed the independence of regional officials, but powerful clans continued to hold power at court and in the regions, and it took centuries for the Chinese-style emperor to take root in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Envoys and students were sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> to learn everything from writing, literature, religion and architecture to eating habits. Chinese civilisation became more deeply entrenched in Japan than China's system of government did.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Taika Reform” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Japan's <b>Taika Reform</b> (645) and Taihō Code (701) explicitly modelled themselves on Tang institutions, and Japanese embassies to the Tang carried monks and students who brought Tang learning home.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Prince Shotoku issued a seventeen-article code in 604, and the <b>Taika reforms</b> of 645 began a centralised land and tax system on Chinese lines.</blockquote><a href="/state/yamato">Yamato</a></li></ul></section>
